<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shetland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shetland Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shetland is an archipelago in the North Atlantic north-east of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, the most northerly part of the United Kingdom. Inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was settled by Norse colonists from the ninth century and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> until 1469, when Christian I pledged it to Scotland as security for his daughter's dowry. Fishing and, since the 1970s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> oil have shaped its economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shetland.jpg" alt="Shetland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Shetland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shetland lies between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, about 80 kilometres northeast of Orkney, 170 kilometres from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and 220 kilometres west of Norway. The largest island, known as the Mainland, is the fifth-largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, and 16 of the islands are inhabited. The climate is oceanic and the coastline rugged, on islands that are virtually treeless. Almost all place names are of Norse origin. The name may derive from the Old Norse hjalt, hilt, and land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans have lived in Shetland since the Mesolithic period. Because people built in stone on treeless islands from at least the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, the islands hold over 5,000 archaeological sites. Norse settlers colonised Shetland in the late 8th and 9th centuries; the fate of the preceding population is unresolved, though there is no established archaeological evidence that settlement involved violent extermination.</p><p>From the mid-13th century Scottish monarchs sought to control the islands around their seas. This led King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV of Norway</a> to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>; after the stalemate at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> he retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and died in December 1263. Under the 1266 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-perth">Treaty of Perth</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and Mann passed to Scotland, but the Scots recognised continuing Norwegian sovereignty over Orkney and Shetland.</p><p>After 1380 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> formed a union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and royal interest in the islands declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> in 1468 and Shetland in 1469 were pledged by Christian I as security for the unpaid dowry of his daughter Margaret on her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a>, with a right of redemption, and the islands were absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1472. Later Danish rulers made repeated attempts to recover them. Shetland kept its own legal system until 1612.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Shetland is the northernmost region of the United Kingdom. Its administrative centre and only burgh is Lerwick, capital since 1708, when it replaced Scalloway. The Norse heritage is reflected in the Up Helly Aa fire festivals and a strong fiddle tradition, and writers have often used the Shetland dialect of Scots. Oil and gas were first landed at Sullom Voe in 1978.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shetland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the sixteenth century Bremen ships traded as far as Iceland and <b>Shetland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Norwegians settled the Faroe Islands, Iceland, <b>Shetland</b>, Orkney, the Hebrides and Man, raided and settled in Ireland and northern Britain, and from Iceland reached Greenland and, around 1000, Newfoundland, where the Norse site at L'Anse aux Meadows has been excavated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Galloway, Moray and the Norse-ruled north and west were brought under royal control only slowly: after the Battle of Largs in 1263, the Treaty of Perth of 1266 transferred the Western Isles and the Isle of Man from Norway to Scotland, and Orkney and <b>Shetland</b> followed in 1468–1469.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The elite displayed its wealth in silver, including massive silver chains and fine penannular brooches; the hoard from St Ninian’s Isle in <b>Shetland</b>, buried around 800, shows the skill of Pictish metalworkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse kings ruled Dublin, founded as a Viking base in 841, and the Isle of Man, and in Orkney and <b>Shetland</b> a descendant of Old Norse, Norn, was spoken until the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse settlers took Orkney, <b>Shetland</b>, the Hebrides and the Isle of Man.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
